WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.509 --> 00:00:16.839
Hello, and welcome to Superstars in History!

2
00:00:17.779 --> 00:00:23.584
I'm your host, Faye Moss, here to interview another famous face from the past.

3
00:00:24.445 --> 00:00:27.328
Today's guest had a very busy career.

4
00:00:28.248 --> 00:00:36.826
He was a priest, lawyer, military general, and eventually the leader of the Roman Republic.

5
00:00:37.807 --> 00:00:41.329
The month of July is even named after him.

6
00:00:42.429 --> 00:00:46.272
Straight from history, here is Julius Caesar!

7
00:01:01.548 --> 00:01:02.489
Thank you, Faye.

8
00:01:03.009 --> 00:01:04.890
What a colorful arena this is!

9
00:01:06.351 --> 00:01:09.412
I can't wait to hear about how you became so famous.

10
00:01:10.333 --> 00:01:15.716
You were born in Rome in 100 BC to a distinguished but poor family.

11
00:01:16.536 --> 00:01:20.178
Your humble beginnings obviously didn't hold you back.

12
00:01:21.239 --> 00:01:21.959
Certainly not.

13
00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:27.742
With my noble ancestry going back to the goddess Venus, I was clearly born to be a leader.

14
00:01:28.683 --> 00:01:32.865
I developed my skills as a speaker so that I could pursue a political career.

15
00:01:33.686 --> 00:01:38.389
When I was a young man, I was appointed high priest of Jupiter, which was a very important

16
00:01:38.469 --> 00:01:44.974
position. However, there were divisions in the republic at the time, and I made some powerful enemies.

17
00:01:45.974 --> 00:01:50.858
I was stripped of my position when I was 19 and fled Rome to save my life.

18
00:01:52.138 --> 00:01:52.859
Oh my!

19
00:01:53.499 --> 00:01:54.780
What did you do next?

20
00:01:55.901 --> 00:02:00.024
I joined the military so I would be far away from my political enemies.

21
00:02:01.052 --> 00:02:04.194
I earned a very important award for my bravery in battle.

22
00:02:04.995 --> 00:02:09.999
By the time I returned to Rome, my enemies were dead, and I was a hero.

23
00:02:10.959 --> 00:02:16.243
I became a lawyer, which gave me a chance to impress the public with my excellent speaking skills.

24
00:02:17.124 --> 00:02:21.747
But I wanted to improve those speaking skills even more so that I could win the hearts of

25
00:02:21.787 --> 00:02:23.728
the people and become a great leader.

26
00:02:24.869 --> 00:02:28.432
I set out for the Greek island of Rhodes to study oratory.

27
00:02:30.056 --> 00:02:33.799
Well, that turned out to be a very exciting trip!

28
00:02:34.959 --> 00:02:37.621
Yes, pirates captured our ship!

29
00:02:38.602 --> 00:02:44.266
Usually these pirates would sell passengers as slaves, but I was much too important for that.

30
00:02:45.166 --> 00:02:48.028
In my case the pirates asked for a ransom in silver.

31
00:02:48.048 --> 00:02:52.091
I was much more valuable than the sum they were demanding.

32
00:02:54.713 --> 00:02:56.574
I told them they should ask for double!

33
00:02:57.595 --> 00:02:58.876
That was bold of you.

34
00:02:59.384 --> 00:03:00.825
But the ransom was paid.

35
00:03:01.825 --> 00:03:02.726
Of course it was.

36
00:03:03.606 --> 00:03:09.229
When I got home, I raised a fleet of warships, found the pirates, and had them all executed.

37
00:03:10.130 --> 00:03:14.612
I am not a barbarian, though, and I ensured that their deaths were quick.

38
00:03:16.974 --> 00:03:23.897
Nonetheless, I taught the world a powerful lesson: It was not wise to provoke Julius Caesar.

39
00:03:25.218 --> 00:03:28.640
Well, you, um, certainly sent a strong message.

40
00:03:29.524 --> 00:03:30.225
Let's move on.

41
00:03:30.685 --> 00:03:33.268
What did you do when you got back to Rome?

42
00:03:34.288 --> 00:03:38.032
I knew the way to gain political power was to have the support of the masses.

43
00:03:39.013 --> 00:03:42.236
I borrowed money so that I could put on a spectacle of wealth.

44
00:03:43.056 --> 00:03:46.099
I hosted public feasts and plays and processions.

45
00:03:48.561 --> 00:03:54.487
I gave many gifts in return for political support and was elected to a series of prestigious positions

46
00:03:54.547 --> 00:03:55.648
in the Roman Republic.

47
00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:00.122
I guess it was okay to give gifts for favors in Rome?

48
00:04:01.103 --> 00:04:02.043
It was successful!

49
00:04:03.024 --> 00:04:09.488
Eventually I needed the support of more powerful people, so I joined with Crassus, one of the wealthiest

50
00:04:09.568 --> 00:04:13.530
men in Rome, and Pompey, a great general and politician.

51
00:04:14.410 --> 00:04:17.272
Together we formed a triumvirate and ruled Rome.

52
00:04:18.233 --> 00:04:23.536
Thanks in no small part to my military skill, we were able to expand Rome's territory.

53
00:04:24.444 --> 00:04:29.206
I conquered all of Gaul and crossed a sea to explore an island in the north.

54
00:04:30.467 --> 00:04:32.928
That island is Great Britain!

55
00:04:33.828 --> 00:04:36.630
And Gaul is now called France and Belgium.

56
00:04:37.850 --> 00:04:41.292
Back at home, though, things weren't exactly going your way.

57
00:04:42.252 --> 00:04:47.214
Crassus was killed in battle, and alas, Pompey turned against me.

58
00:04:48.195 --> 00:04:52.680
By now, my term of service had come to an end, and the senate ordered me to give

59
00:04:52.760 --> 00:04:56.141
up my command and return to Rome as a private citizen.

60
00:04:57.082 --> 00:04:59.522
I returned, and I brought my army with me.

61
00:05:00.663 --> 00:05:03.643
Had I returned alone, my enemies would have killed me.

62
00:05:04.544 --> 00:05:10.405
Pompey fled in fear, and within a year, I was the sole dictator of Rome.

63
00:05:12.306 --> 00:05:16.287
In ancient Rome "dictator" meant something different than it does today.

64
00:05:16.307 --> 00:05:20.428
It was an important political position in the Roman Republic, right?

65
00:05:21.608 --> 00:05:26.810
Yes, a dictator was appointed by the senate to lead Rome in times of emergency.

66
00:05:27.771 --> 00:05:31.092
It was a powerful position but had limited authority.

67
00:05:32.112 --> 00:05:39.375
After I defeated Pompey in Egypt and eliminated his supporters, I was named dictator for life.

68
00:05:40.536 --> 00:05:46.518
In Egypt you met Cleopatra and established good relations between Egypt and Rome.

69
00:05:47.278 --> 00:05:53.048
But I'm curious about something else you encountered there: the calendar.

70
00:05:53.869 --> 00:06:01.211
Your 12-month calendar with 365 days is the basis for the calendar we use today.

71
00:06:02.631 --> 00:06:11.153
Rome had a lunar calendar with 355 days, and it required confusing adjustments of extra days or months

72
00:06:11.233 --> 00:06:12.114
to stay regular.

73
00:06:13.014 --> 00:06:16.715
The Egyptians used a simpler solar calendar with 365 days.

74
00:06:16.735 --> 00:06:23.229
I was impressed by it and implemented a similar calendar in Rome.

75
00:06:24.109 --> 00:06:29.192
It was a great success, and of course, named after me: the Julian Calendar.

76
00:06:30.112 --> 00:06:33.693
And later the month of July was named for you too.

77
00:06:34.574 --> 00:06:39.876
You were widely seen as a reformer, and the calendar wasn't your only reform.

78
00:06:41.757 --> 00:06:42.797
What else did you do?

79
00:06:44.018 --> 00:06:49.000
I extended citizenship to many foreigners and allowed Jews to practice their faith.

80
00:06:49.960 --> 00:06:56.263
I also tried to make a fairer society by giving land to poor families and changing the rules

81
00:06:56.324 --> 00:06:57.484
of property ownership.

82
00:06:58.365 --> 00:07:01.186
I even shared the plunders of war with the citizens.

83
00:07:02.087 --> 00:07:04.348
I was very popular with the common people.

84
00:07:05.208 --> 00:07:08.070
You weren't popular with everyone, though.

85
00:07:09.110 --> 00:07:15.134
Many in the senate feared your power and were troubled that you were now in charge for life.

86
00:07:16.354 --> 00:07:21.273
That is true, and to please them, I tried to stay humble.

87
00:07:22.234 --> 00:07:25.316
I refused a crown and refused to be called king.

88
00:07:26.216 --> 00:07:30.940
I'm not sure if my position and power were the only reasons some people opposed me.

89
00:07:31.820 --> 00:07:34.702
Some of my decisions as ruler greatly upset people.

90
00:07:35.503 --> 00:07:40.366
I gave land and power to the poor and attempted to restrict the amount of wealth a single

91
00:07:40.466 --> 00:07:41.767
individual could amass.

92
00:07:42.688 --> 00:07:46.670
Because of this, I knew aristocrats in the senate were plotting against me.

93
00:07:47.511 --> 00:07:48.992
But I wasn't too worried.

94
00:07:49.960 --> 00:07:53.743
I'm not sure if it was wise not to worry.

95
00:07:54.663 --> 00:07:55.904
Maybe we should end here.

96
00:07:56.925 --> 00:07:58.606
Thank you for joining us today.

97
00:07:59.507 --> 00:08:01.908
We'll let you return to history now.

98
00:08:02.969 --> 00:08:04.250
It was my pleasure, Faye.

99
00:08:07.352 --> 00:08:15.118
In 44 BC on the fifteenth of March—or the Ides of March as the Romans called it— a

100
00:08:15.178 --> 00:08:20.646
group of senators unhappy with Caesar's rule surprised him at the senate.

101
00:08:21.727 --> 00:08:26.532
They stabbed him to death, and he died on the senate floor.

102
00:08:27.492 --> 00:08:33.458
Caesar's killers said they murdered him to save the republic, but the result was quite different.

103
00:08:34.399 --> 00:08:40.905
Rome descended into civil war, and the Roman Republic eventually became the Roman Empire.

104
00:08:42.146 --> 00:08:43.868
Thank you for joining me today!

105
00:08:44.724 --> 00:08:49.914
I'll see you next time on another episode of Superstars in History.
